          up: {"title":"Spot the netiquette errors (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A supervisor at <b>Antigua Office Supplies</b> drafted this reply to a customer complaint. There are no capital letters this time; the problems are more subtle.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that breaks netiquette or safe practice.","lines":["1. Dear Mr. Benjamin","2. Thank you for your e-mail of Monday 19 October about the damaged printer.","3. Well, we are not mind-readers, so next time maybe say which model you bought. 🙄","4. I have attached the delivery note, which shows the printer was signed for on Wednesday 21 October.","5. As you can see from Ms. Thomas's internal notes below, she thinks you broke it yourself.","6. To process a refund quickly, just reply with your online banking password.","7. For setup help, see this video I found: bit.ly/fr33-printer-fix","8. I will call you by Friday 23 October to arrange a replacement.","9. Kind regards, Kerry-Ann James, Sales Supervisor"],"correct":[2,4,5,6],"win":"Excellent judgement. Line 3 is sarcastic and unprofessional, line 5 forwards a colleague's private internal notes, line 6 asks for a password (a genuine business never does), and line 7 sends an unverified shortened link to an unknown site. The other lines are polite, accurate and helpful.","miss":"Look beyond capital letters. Check each line for tone (sarcasm, emojis in a formal reply), privacy (internal notes), security (passwords) and safety (unknown links)."}
