          up: {"title":"Good netiquette or poor netiquette? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>These workplace habits are harder to judge. Think about tone, privacy, safety, intellectual property and timeliness.</p>","items":[{"t":"Replying \"Thanks!\" with Reply All on a thread sent to 150 staff.","bin":1,"fb":"It fills 150 inboxes with a message only one person needed. Reply to the sender only."},{"t":"Writing \"Urgent\" in a subject line only when a same-day action is genuinely needed.","bin":0,"fb":"Using urgency honestly keeps it meaningful, so readers trust it when it appears."},{"t":"Checking a \"bank\" e-mail's sender address and the real destination of its link before clicking anything.","bin":0,"fb":"Verifying suspicious messages before acting on them is safe practice."},{"t":"Forwarding a funny video of a customer filmed in the shop without her permission.","bin":1,"fb":"This breaks the customer's privacy, and the video could spread far beyond the office."},{"t":"Using a colleague's design in a presentation and crediting her by name.","bin":0,"fb":"Crediting the creator respects intellectual property."},{"t":"Sending an angry reply at 11:00 p.m. and apologising the next morning.","bin":1,"fb":"Once sent, the message cannot be retracted. Wait, cool down and proofread before replying."},{"t":"Answering a client's detailed question with \"Received. I will send a full answer by Thursday.\"","bin":0,"fb":"Acknowledging receipt and setting a time for the full reply is good netiquette."},{"t":"Keeping a sensitive staff complaint out of the team chat and discussing it by phone with HR.","bin":0,"fb":"Sensitive information belongs on a private, appropriate channel."},{"t":"Sharing your login with a temporary worker so she can check the shared inbox.","bin":1,"fb":"Login details must never be shared; the temporary worker should be given her own authorised access."}],"win":"Sound judgement. The hardest cards were Reply All, the late-night reply and the shared login: each seems harmless or helpful, but each breaks a netiquette or security rule."}
